---
/**
 * Guide — what cairn is, why it exists, and what each role does with it.
 *
 * Read by designers and developers at the same time, which is why the two
 * sections that belong to one of them carry that role's mark: a reader should be
 * able to find their own stretch of the page without reading the other one.
 *
 * This page argues. Every command in the system lives on `/getting-started`,
 * which instructs — the one exception is the designer's three commands below,
 * because "it runs on its own" is a claim, and they are the evidence for it.
 */
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro'
import PageIntro from '../components/PageIntro.astro'
import Section from '../components/Section.astro'
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro'
import LabelledList from '../components/LabelledList.astro'
import RoleBadge from '../components/RoleBadge.astro'
import Text from '../components/ui/Text.astro'
import Code from '../components/ui/Code.astro'
import CodeBlock from '../components/ui/CodeBlock.astro'
import { metrics } from '../fixtures/metrics'

const DESIGN_COMMANDS = `npm run design         # see it in the browser, reloading as you work
npm run design:check   # check it against the house rules
npm run design:build   # static HTML to send to a client`

const NOT_YOUR_JOB = [
  'What a component is called.',
  'Where a file goes.',
  'What a token, a prop or a variant is.',
  'Git.',
]

const ASKED_INSTEAD = [
  'What this screen is for.',
  'Who opens it, and what they are trying to do.',
  'The real words on the buttons.',
  'What happens on click.',
  'What is on screen when the list is empty, while it loads, and when something breaks.',
]
---

<Page
  title="Marks on the trail — cairn"
  description="cairn is the set of rules and tools that lets the people who design write frontend the people who build want to inherit."
>
  <PageIntro
    title="Marks on the trail"
    lead="cairn is the set of rules and tools that lets the people who design write frontend the people who build want to inherit."
  >
    <Text class="mt-6 max-w-2xl">
      Designers produce code. That code becomes the developers' starting point — and the context
      their agents read. If it is disordered, the disorder is paid for twice. cairn lays the rails
      first instead of patching afterwards.
    </Text>
  </PageIntro>

  <div class="mt-16 space-y-16">
    <Section title="A project does not break all at once">
      <Text class="max-w-2xl">
        It breaks by accumulation. A <Code>13px</Code> typed by hand, a component copied and
        adjusted, a colour it was quicker to paste than to name. Each defensible on its own.
        Together they produce a project where nothing can be changed in one place, and where
        whoever arrives next — person or agent — has no way to tell the rule from the exception.
      </Text>

      <div class="mt-8 border-t border-rule">
        {metrics.map((metric) => <Figure value={metric.value} label={metric.label} />)}
      </div>

      <Text variant="lead" class="mt-8 max-w-2xl font-display">
        None of this is incompetence. It is the absence of a rule.
      </Text>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A folder that runs on its own">
      <Text class="max-w-2xl">
        Designers work in <Code>design/</Code>, a standalone project that starts with one command:
        no backend, no database, no docker. Developers take those components into the real project.
        Both follow the same composition rules, because they are building the same thing.
      </Text>

      <Text class="mt-5 max-w-2xl">
        <Code>design/</Code> is not a phase, it is permanent. Designers keep correcting it while
        development runs — that is how it goes, and pretending otherwise helps nobody. So it is a
        living source, and production is a copy that drifts from it. A script says how far. Nobody
        hunts for it by eye.
      </Text>

      <CodeBlock class="mt-8" label="the three commands" code={DESIGN_COMMANDS} />
    </Section>

    <Section title="If you design" tone="designer">
      <RoleBadge slot="eyebrow" role="designer" />

      <Text class="max-w-2xl">
        Open the <Code>design/</Code> folder and type <Code>/design-workflow</Code>. Describe what
        you want. You will be asked questions you can answer — what the screen is for, what it
        says, what is on it before there is anything — and never questions about code.
      </Text>

      <div class="mt-8 grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-2">
        <LabelledList title="what you do not need to know" items={NOT_YOUR_JOB} tone="designer" />
        <LabelledList title="what you are asked instead" items={ASKED_INSTEAD} tone="designer" />
      </div>

      <Text class="mt-8 max-w-2xl">
        Everything outside <Code>design/</Code> belongs to the developers. Needing something out
        there is a two-minute conversation, not a problem to work around.
      </Text>
    </Section>

    <Section title="If you develop" tone="developer">
      <RoleBadge slot="eyebrow" role="developer" />

      <Text class="max-w-2xl">
        The design is not a picture: it is code that runs, that you did not write, and that you are
        not free to ignore. That is what makes this faster than working from mockups — and it stays
        faster only while the two copies keep matching.
      </Text>

      <Text class="mt-5 max-w-2xl">
        So you read the handoff, then triage before touching anything: keep, normalize, rewrite. It
        is a two-minute table and the cheapest moment there is to discover that four files need
        rewriting.
      </Text>

      <Text class="mt-5 max-w-2xl">
        And what production learns travels back. A field that can be empty, real content longer
        than any fixture, a state nobody drew. If it does not travel, <Code>design/</Code> becomes
        fiction within two months — and a workbench nobody trusts is a workbench nobody maintains.
      </Text>
    </Section>

    <Section title="The order of things">
      <Text class="max-w-2xl">
        Who does what, when, the commands given once, and how a component gets from the workbench
        into the real project: that is the next mark on the trail, and the page to keep open on the
        first day of a new project.
      </Text>
    </Section>
  </div>
</Page>
